    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "63e62f96_80dea222",
        "filename": "driver/usb_mux/usb_mux.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 366,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-13T17:25:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, we could go that route.  Setting a uint32_t to 0 is essentially atomic since there\u0027s no read/modify/write, right? Just for my reference\n\nAny preference between still clearing all flags, or just clearing the ones I know I care about (init and LPM)?",
      "parentUuid": "91160edb_e4157d3d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 366,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 366,
        "endChar": 17
      },
      "revId": "d57d8602baa92efbddbd1dbd008ad42c6de53932",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "79849200_fad84449",
        "filename": "driver/usb_mux/usb_mux.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 366,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-13T19:23:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "On a single core system, atomic_clear() is equivalent to setting the variable directly to 0.  Using atomic_clear() is really defensive coding if ever we switched to a SMP implementation.\n\nIt\u0027s probably best to just clear the INIT and LPM flags.  If another flag is added, it may not be obvious that board code might clear it.",
      "parentUuid": "63e62f96_80dea222",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 366,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 366,
        "endChar": 17
      },
